BUILD_ONLY: ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/efe-private:labutopia-<images/labutopia/VERSION> is never public (docs/protocol/README.md
§1, base images). It is kept in the organization's private package as the build cache of the LabUtopia tasks: every task's
Dockerfile starts with this recipe (images/labutopia/Dockerfile, copied in by scripts/task_conventions.py normalize) and
its compose names the private image in `build.cache_from`. A runner that can pull the image takes its layers; any other
runner builds the same recipe from public sources on its first task (`harbor run` does it; nothing to run by hand).

Why
  The image contains NVIDIA Isaac Sim 6.1.0 / Omniverse Kit (pip wheels from pypi.nvidia.com), which NVIDIA licenses
  for use, not for redistribution, and 64 files from NVIDIA's public asset server (images/labutopia/
  nvidia_assets.sha256: Isaac Sim's Franka robot model, the cabinet LabUtopia's laboratory stage references in the
  Isaac 4.2 asset tree, four materials of the Omniverse material library, one sample file). LabUtopia
  (github.com/Rui-li023/LabUtopia @ 8df7278) is MIT for its code and CC BY-NC 4.0 for its assets (0.36 GB through git
  LFS, baked in): research and educational use only.

What the builder accepts
  - the NVIDIA Isaac Sim / Omniverse license agreement (the image sets OMNI_KIT_ACCEPT_EULA=YES and ACCEPT_EULA=Y, and
    every container that runs it accepts it again at launch);
  - NVIDIA's terms for the isaacsim wheels on pypi.nvidia.com and for the Isaac Sim and Omniverse assets;
  - LabUtopia's MIT licence and the CC BY-NC 4.0 licence of its assets.
  No account, key or token is needed: everything is downloaded anonymously.

Isaac Sim 6.1.0, not LabUtopia's 5.1.0
  LabUtopia's README installs Isaac Sim 5.1.0 (Python 3.11). This image installs 6.1.0 (Python 3.12). On the host the
  benchmark was built on (RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition, NVIDIA driver 595.91.07) Isaac Sim 5.1.0 crashes at
  start-up in librtx.scenedb.plugin.so, a known incompatibility of the R595 driver branch for which NVIDIA's answer is
  the 580 branch (github.com/isaac-sim/IsaacSim discussions/648, issues/619); 6.1.0 is the first release whose
  requirements list an R595 driver (595.58.03 on Linux). LabUtopia's code at the pinned commit runs on it unchanged:
  the three adjustments 6.1 needs are in the tooling (each task's rcb_labutopia.py, `_compat`). The Python
  environment is Isaac Sim 6.1.0's own (numpy 2.3, torch 2.11) plus hydra-core and omegaconf at LabUtopia's pins and
  h5py, pandas, zarr, dill, einops, tqdm and imageio-ffmpeg at versions that go with it; LabUtopia's other pins
  (numpy 1.26 and its policy-training packages) are not installed. The frozen instances, the reference trajectories
  and every recorded result are for this image. Not checked: Isaac Sim 6.1.0 with a 580-branch driver.

What is not in the image
  LabUtopia's scripted controllers, their motion primitives and its policy code (controllers/, policy/,
  data_collectors/, main.py and the two factories that build them): images/labutopia/strip_solutions.py removes them
  in the layer that clones the repository and the build fails if the check afterwards finds one. They are the
  benchmark's reference solutions.

Host
  Linux x86_64, Docker with the NVIDIA Container Toolkit, an RTX-class NVIDIA GPU with a driver Isaac Sim 6.1.0
  supports (NVIDIA lists 595.58.03 on Linux; checked on 595.91.07 with an RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition).
  Isaac Sim renders with RTX; PhysX runs on the CPU. Build: about 90 GB of free disk, network access to github.com (+
  its LFS), astral.sh, pypi.org, pypi.nvidia.com and omniverse-content-production.s3-us-west-2.amazonaws.com; about 25
  minutes on a fast link. The image is 40 GB. Run: 32 GB of RAM per simulator that renders (the first simulator with
  its renderer in a container prepares the stage for it: 5 to 15 minutes on 8 idle cores, up to 21 GB of RAM; a simulator
  without the renderer takes about 6 GB). Nothing is fetched at run time: the tasks run without network.

Build and publish (maintainers; from the repository root)
  RCB_PUSH_PRIVATE=1 scripts/build_images.sh builds every image (this one with BuildKit's inline cache); alone:
    docker build --build-arg BUILDKIT_INLINE_CACHE=1 -t ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/efe-private:labutopia-$(cat images/labutopia/VERSION) images/labutopia
    docker push ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/efe-private:labutopia-$(cat images/labutopia/VERSION)
  A new VERSION: change the tag in the tasks' `cache_from` and normalize them.

Check a build
  harbor run -p tasks/labutopia/labutopia-pick-i00-privileged -a oracle (reward 1): the verifier rebuilds the frozen
  instance and replays the reference trajectory in two fresh processes: the placements must equal the recorded ones,
  and both replays must succeed and end in one state. On the host the benchmark was built
  on they end in the recorded state as well; PhysX can differ in the last bits between hosts (tasks/robolab/README.md
  has seen it), in which case the check says so and a trial is graded on the host it ran on.
